    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION: Positioning -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="positioning">
      <h2>What This Study Is and Is Not</h2>

      <p>This study is a <strong>qualitative brand intelligence snapshot</strong> that uses structured interviews at scale. It is not a brand health tracker. Understanding this distinction shapes how we position the study to clients, design the instrument, and interpret results.</p>

      <div class="comparison-grid">
        <div class="comparison-card b2b">
          <h3>Brand Health Tracker (BBH)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Longitudinal quantitative survey</li>
            <li>Prompted brand lists, forced-choice responses</li>
            <li>Tracker-grade metrics (MMS, MPen, NS, SOM)</li>
            <li>Statistically representative samples</li>
            <li>Tells you <strong>WHAT</strong> is happening</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="comparison-question">"Brand awareness dropped 3 points."</p>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-card b2c">
          <h3>Our Interview Study</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>One-time qualitative snapshot at scale (200 interviews)</li>
            <li>Open-ended questions, AI-moderated</li>
            <li>Directional metrics (not tracker-precision)</li>
            <li>Consumer's own language, reasoning, emotional response</li>
            <li>Tells you <strong>WHY</strong> it's happening</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="comparison-question">"Non-customers associate you with 'old-fashioned banking' in the everyday spending CEP."</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Three Roles This Study Plays</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Client Situation</th><th>What This Study Provides</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Has a tracker</strong></td>
              <td>Explains WHY tracker metrics move. The tracker shows awareness dropped; our interviews reveal the reason.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>No tracker yet</strong></td>
              <td>Foundational understanding to design one. Our CEP identification provides the initial CEP list and baselines.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Entering a new market</strong></td>
              <td>Initial landscape map: who competitors are in consumers' minds, the CEP structure, and mental availability gaps.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>What this study uniquely offers (things surveys cannot capture):</strong> (1) Confirmation with richness: validates known patterns (Double Jeopardy, low rejection) with qualitative detail explaining WHY they hold. (2) New discoveries: consumer language, origin stories, switching narratives, and CEP associations that no closed-ended survey can capture. (3) New frameworks: the CEP landscape becomes the tracker's measurement framework; segment profiles become targeting strategy.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION: Why Different -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="why-different">
      <h2>Why B2C Requires a Different Approach</h2>

      <p>The B2B methodology (built for the HR Leaders BGR study) is structured around a <strong>deliberate evaluation process</strong>: a buyer had a trigger, formed a consideration set, compared alternatives against criteria, involved a buying committee, and chose a winner. Module A reconstructs that evaluation story.</p>

      <p>In B2C, the buying process is fundamentally different. The interview architecture must change to match.</p>

      <div class="comparison-grid">
        <div class="comparison-card b2b">
          <h3>B2B (Current Approach)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Every buyer went through a deliberate evaluation</li>
            <li>Buying committees with multiple stakeholders</li>
            <li>Formal criteria, demos, and vendor comparisons</li>
            <li>Contracts expire and force periodic re-evaluation</li>
            <li>JTBD switching analysis applies to the full sample</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="comparison-question">"How did you decide?"</p>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-card b2c">
          <h3>B2C (New Approach)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Most consumers never "evaluated" their provider</li>
            <li>Individual or household decisions</li>
            <li>Inherited relationships, proximity, life events</li>
            <li>Switching is rare, friction-laden, and inertia-heavy</li>
            <li>Mental availability is the primary growth driver</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="comparison-question">"How did you end up here, and what would make you leave?"</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Four Structural Changes from B2B</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>No Module A / Module B split.</strong> In B2C, ~70-80% of participants have no evaluation story. We use a four-section structure instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Mental landscape comes first.</strong> CEP identification and brand associations (Section 1) precede the participant identifying their own bank (Section 2) to prevent anchoring bias in open-ended recall.</li>
        <li><strong>CEP identification is the strategic core.</strong> The CEP block (Q3-Q4) is the most time-intensive section and produces the most strategically valuable output. No B2B equivalent.</li>
        <li><strong>The switching question is branched.</strong> Only ~20-30% have switched. Q12 branches: switchers get a JTBD deep dive, non-switchers get retention anchors.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION: Research Foundations -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="research-foundations">
      <h2>Theoretical Foundations</h2>

      <p>Every element of this interview is grounded in at least one validated academic framework. No invented methods. This section documents which frameworks inform the study and how they connect to specific interview sections.</p>

      <div class="foundations-grid">
        <div class="foundation-card">
          <h4>1. Ehrenberg-Bass / How Brands Grow</h4>
          <p class="foundation-source">Sharp (2010), Romaniuk &amp; Sharp (2022)</p>
          <p>Mental availability drives growth. Brands grow by penetration (reaching more non-buyers), not loyalty. Rejection is low (7-11%); unawareness is 4-16x larger. Growth comes from non-buyers. Every metric must be split by buyer/non-buyer (BBH Ch. 2, 6, 7).</p>
          <p class="foundation-implication"><strong>Interview:</strong> Q2 awareness, Q3-Q4 CEPs, Q5 rejection, buyer/non-buyer framework</p>
        </div>
        <div class="foundation-card">
          <h4>2. Category Entry Points &amp; Ws Framework</h4>
          <p class="foundation-source">Romaniuk &amp; Sharp (2022), Ch. 5-6</p>
          <p>CEPs are the situational cues that trigger brand recall. The Ws framework (Why, When, Where, While, Whom, What, hoW feeling) structures CEP identification. Six design principles: open-ended cue, Ws probes, completeness push, diverse buyers, balance breadth/distinctiveness, periodic refresh.</p>
          <p class="foundation-implication"><strong>Interview:</strong> Q3 (genuine CEP identification with Ws probes) and Q4 (brand-CEP associations)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="foundation-card">
          <h4>3. Customer-Based Brand Equity (CBBE)</h4>
          <p class="foundation-source">Keller (1993, 2001)</p>
          <p>Brand equity = awareness (recall + recognition) + image (associations varying in strength, favorability, uniqueness). Direction coding measures Keller's "favorability" dimension. Three-tier awareness derives from recall (Q2) and CEP linkage (Q4).</p>
          <p class="foundation-implication"><strong>Interview:</strong> Q2 (recall), Q4 (associations + favorability via direction coding)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="foundation-card">
          <h4>4. JTBD Switching Forces</h4>
          <p class="foundation-source">Christensen (1997), Moesta &amp; Spiek (2014)</p>
          <p>Four forces: push (dissatisfaction), pull (attraction of new), anxiety (fear of change), habit (comfort). Switching when push + pull > anxiety + habit. In banking, anxiety and habit dominate. Intensity coding reveals which forces drive behavior vs. passive mention.</p>
          <p class="foundation-implication"><strong>Interview:</strong> Q12 (JTBD forces, branched by switching history)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="foundation-card">
          <h4>5. Voice of the Customer (VoC)</h4>
          <p class="foundation-source">Griffin &amp; Hauser (1993)</p>
          <p>20-30 interviews capture ~90% of customer needs. Our 200 far exceed this, enabling segmentation and cross-brand comparison. Value is in the customer's own language, not corporate jargon. Feeds the Consumer Language Bank.</p>
          <p class="foundation-implication"><strong>Interview:</strong> Q9 (retained value), Q10 (frustrations), Q11 (unmet needs), Q16 (ideal experience)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="foundation-card">
          <h4>6. Market Segmentation</h4>
          <p class="foundation-source">Wedel &amp; Kamakura (2000)</p>
          <p>Basis variables (form segments), descriptor variables (profile them), criterion variables (validate them). Terminology follows Wedel &amp; Kamakura exactly. Every question classified by segmentation role.</p>
          <p class="foundation-implication"><strong>Interview:</strong> Every question pre-classified as basis, descriptor, or criterion</p>
        </div>
        <div class="foundation-card full-width">
          <h4>7. Magnitude Coding</h4>
          <p class="foundation-source">Saldana (2021), Miles, Huberman &amp; Saldana (2020), Osgood et al. (1956), Krippendorff (2018)</p>
          <p>Adds supplemental codes for "intensity, frequency, direction, presence, or evaluative content" (Saldana, 2021). We apply two dimensions: <strong>direction</strong> (+/-/~) at meaning-unit level and <strong>intensity</strong> (1/2/3) at participant x code level. All terminology is Saldana's exact terms. Direction has a 70+ year research tradition in content analysis. Intensity predicts switching behavior (Singh 1988, Zeelenberg &amp; Pieters 2004).</p>
          <p class="foundation-implication"><strong>Interview:</strong> Applied to ALL meaning units across ALL thematic questions</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION: Recruitment -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="recruitment">
      <h2>Recruitment and Quota Design</h2>

      <p>The recommended approach mirrors the B2B brand-cohort model: recruit by primary provider relationship, with quotas ensuring coverage of specific brands the client cares about.</p>

      <h3>Recommended Quota Structure (~200 Participants)</h3>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Cohort</th><th>Target N</th><th>Rationale</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Client's brand</strong></td>
              <td>60</td>
              <td>Deep understanding of own customers. Largest cohort for robust buyer analysis.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Competitor A</strong></td>
              <td>40</td>
              <td>Head-to-head comparison with a named rival</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Competitor B</strong></td>
              <td>40</td>
              <td>Second named rival for triangulation</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Other traditional players</strong></td>
              <td>30</td>
              <td>Fills out the traditional provider landscape</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Digital challengers</strong></td>
              <td>30</td>
              <td>Captures the disruptor segment</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="total-row">
              <td><strong>Total</strong></td>
              <td><strong>200</strong></td>
              <td></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Buyer/Non-Buyer Sample Sizes</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>For Analysis of...</th><th>Buyers</th><th>Non-Buyers</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Client's bank</td><td>~60</td><td>~140</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Competitor A</td><td>~40</td><td>~160</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Competitor B</td><td>~40</td><td>~160</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Other traditional</td><td>~30</td><td>~170</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Digital challengers</td><td>~30 total (thin per brand)</td><td>~170</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Demographics to Capture in Screener</h3>
      <ul class="detail-list">
        <li><strong>Age band:</strong> 18-25 / 26-35 / 36-50 / 51-65 / 65+</li>
        <li><strong>Province</strong> (banking behavior varies; Quebec has Desjardins)</li>
        <li><strong>Area type:</strong> Urban / suburban / rural (branch access matters)</li>
        <li><strong>Household income band</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Multi-provider usage:</strong> Whether they use more than one bank</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Why brand cohorts over random sampling:</strong> Random sampling overrepresents market leaders and underrepresents challengers. Quotas ensure enough participants per brand for meaningful cross-brand comparisons and buyer/non-buyer splits with sufficient sample sizes.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION: Interview Framework -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="interview">
      <h2>Interview Framework (16 Questions, 20 Minutes)</h2>

      <p>Four sections, 16 questions. Ordered to prevent anchoring bias: mental landscape captured BEFORE the participant identifies their own bank. Every question produces at least one basis variable, report metric, or recommendation-driving insight.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Design principle: Mental landscape before personal story.</strong> CEP identification and brand associations (Section 1) come before the participant identifies their own bank (Section 2). Asking "which bank is yours?" before open-ended brand recall questions primes that brand in working memory, inflating its CEP associations. In surveys with prompted brand lists this is less of a concern, but in open-ended interviews it matters.
      </div>

      <div class="time-budget">
        <div class="time-block">
          <span class="time-label">Section 1</span>
          <span class="time-name">Mental Landscape</span>
          <span class="time-duration">8-9 min</span>
          <div class="time-bar" style="width: 42.5%"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="time-block">
          <span class="time-label">Section 2</span>
          <span class="time-name">Your Banking Story</span>
          <span class="time-duration">3-4 min</span>
          <div class="time-bar section2" style="width: 17.5%"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="time-block">
          <span class="time-label">Section 3</span>
          <span class="time-name">Needs, Friction &amp; Switching</span>
          <span class="time-duration">5-6 min</span>
          <div class="time-bar section3" style="width: 27.5%"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="time-block">
          <span class="time-label">Section 4</span>
          <span class="time-name">The Bigger Picture</span>
          <span class="time-duration">1-2 min</span>
          <div class="time-bar section4" style="width: 7.5%"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 1: Mental Landscape -->
      <div class="interview-section">
        <h3>Section 1: Mental Landscape <span class="section-time">8-9 min</span></h3>
        <p class="section-purpose">Captures the participant's mental map of the banking category BEFORE they identify their own bank. Spontaneous awareness, CEP identification, brand-CEP associations, and rejection measured while working memory is unanchored. Three-tier awareness derived from Q2 + Q4.</p>

        <div class="question-list">
          <div class="question">
            <div class="q-header">
              <span class="q-num">Q1</span>
              <span class="q-role descriptor">Descriptor</span>
            </div>
            <p class="q-text">"To start, could you tell me your age range, which province you live in, and whether you'd describe your area as urban, suburban, or rural?"</p>
            <p class="q-coding">Discrete categories: age band, province, area type. Not magnitude coded (categorical data).</p>
          </div>

          <div class="question">
            <div class="q-header">
              <span class="q-num">Q2</span>
              <span class="q-role metric">Report Metric</span>
            </div>
            <p class="q-text">"When you think about banks in Canada, which ones come to mind?"</p>
            <p class="q-coding">Code all banks mentioned, in order. First-mentioned = TOM. This is spontaneous awareness (recall) per Keller's CBBE. Combined with Q4, produces the <strong>three-tier awareness classification</strong>: (1) Mentally available (mentioned in Q4 CEP responses), (2) Aware but not linked (mentioned in Q2 but not Q4), (3) Unaware (not mentioned anywhere). <strong>Direction</strong> coded on any evaluative comments alongside brand names.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="question">
            <div class="q-header">
              <span class="q-num">Q3</span>
              <span class="q-role basis">CEP Identification</span>
            </div>
            <p class="q-text">"Think about a time recently when banking or finances came to mind. What was happening? What were you doing, or what prompted you to think about your bank or a bank?"</p>
            <div class="cep-battery">
              <h4>AI Moderator Probes (Ws Framework, BBH Ch. 5)</h4>
              <div class="cep-list">
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">Why</span>
                  <p>"What specifically prompted that thought? Was it a need, a frustration, a goal?"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">When</span>
                  <p>"Are there particular times when banking comes to mind more?"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">Where</span>
                  <p>"Where were you? At home, at work, in a store, on your phone?"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">While</span>
                  <p>"What else were you doing at the time?"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">Whom</span>
                  <p>"Was anyone else involved? Banking for yourself, family, someone else?"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">What</span>
                  <p>"What specific product or service were you thinking about?"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">hoW</span>
                  <p>"How were you feeling? Stressed, confident, frustrated, neutral?"</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="q-coding">This is genuine CEP identification per BBH's recommended methodology (Ch. 5). Code each distinct situation as a potential Category Entry Point. Use 2-3 Ws probes based on participant's response, push once for completeness. 3-4 minutes. <strong>Direction + intensity</strong> coded.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="question">
            <div class="q-header">
              <span class="q-num">Q4</span>
              <span class="q-role basis">Basis + Report Metric</span>
            </div>
            <p class="q-text">"For each of these situations, tell me which bank or banks come to mind first, and why."</p>
            <div class="cep-battery">
              <h4>CEP Battery (4-5 situations, drawing from Q3 + pre-set CEPs)</h4>
              <div class="cep-list">
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">a</span>
                  <p>"Everyday spending and transactions (bills, groceries, transfers)"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">b</span>
                  <p>"Mortgage or buying a home"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">c</span>
                  <p>"Savings or investing for the future"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">d</span>
                  <p>"Digital banking (everything from phone/computer)"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">e</span>
                  <p>"Financial advice for a major decision"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="cep-item">
                  <span class="cep-letter">f</span>
                  <p>"Recommending a bank to someone new to Canada"</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="q-coding">For each CEP: first-mentioned bank, all banks, reason for association. Produces the <strong>CEP-Brand Association Map</strong>. <strong>Direction</strong> captures Keller's favorability dimension. <strong>Intensity</strong> at participant x code level. <strong>Split by buyer/non-buyer</strong> in analysis. Non-buyer data is the strategic data for growth.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="question">
            <div class="q-header">
              <span class="q-num">Q5</span>
              <span class="q-role metric">Report Metric</span>
            </div>
            <p class="q-text">"Are there any banks you would absolutely not consider, regardless of the situation? Which ones, and why?"</p>
            <p class="q-coding">Produces <strong>Brand Rejection Rate</strong> per brand. Ehrenberg-Bass predicts 7-11%. Direction inherently negative. <strong>Most meaningful among non-buyers</strong>. Rejection is a separate overlay on the three-tier awareness classification.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 2: Banking Story -->
      <div class="interview-section">
        <h3>Section 2: Your Banking Story <span class="section-time">3-4 min</span></h3>
        <p class="section-purpose">Now that the mental landscape is captured, the participant identifies their own bank and tells their origin story. Q6 is the KEY variable that enables buyer/non-buyer analysis for all brand-level metrics.</p>

        <div class="question-list">
          <div class="question">
            <div class="q-header">
              <span class="q-num">Q6</span>
              <span class="q-role criterion">Criterion + Key Classifier</span>
            </div>
            <p class="q-text">"Which bank do you consider your primary bank, the one where you do most of your day-to-day banking? And roughly how long have you been with them?"</p>
            <p class="q-coding"><strong>CRITICAL: This is the KEY buyer/non-buyer classifier for ALL brand-level analysis.</strong> Primary bank is the criterion variable (never clustering input). Tenure as ordinal bands. Not magnitude coded.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="question">
            <div class="q-header">
              <span class="q-num">Q7</span>
              <span class="q-role basis">Basis</span>
            </div>
            <p class="q-text">"How did you first end up at this bank? What's the story behind how you became a customer there?"</p>
            <p class="q-coding">Thematic: <strong>Bank Origin</strong> pathway (inherited / proximity / life event / actively chose / employer / recommendation / other). <strong>Basis variable.</strong> <strong>Direction + intensity</strong> coded: "I specifically chose them" (+) vs. "there was no other option" (-).</p>
          </div>

          <div class="question">
            <div class="q-header">
              <span class="q-num">Q8</span>
              <span class="q-role basis">Basis</span>
            </div>
            <p class="q-text">"Besides your primary bank, do you use any other banks or financial services? If so, which ones and what do you use them for?"</p>
            <p class="q-coding">Multi-banking binary (<strong>basis variable</strong>) + thematic reasons for splitting. <strong>Direction + intensity</strong> coded.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 3: Needs & Switching -->
      <div class="interview-section">
        <h3>Section 3: Needs, Friction, and Switching <span class="section-time">5-6 min</span></h3>
        <p class="section-purpose">What consumers value, what frustrates them, and the forces anchoring or pushing them. Branches on switching history. Magnitude coding on all meaning units. Buyer/non-buyer split on frustrations.</p>

        <div class="question-list">
          <div class="question">
            <div class="q-header">
              <span class="q-num">Q9</span>
              <span class="q-role descriptor">Descriptor</span>
            </div>
            <p class="q-text">"What does your primary bank do particularly well for you? What would you miss most if you had to leave?"</p>
            <p class="q-coding">Retained value themes. <strong>Direction</strong> inherently positive. <strong>Intensity</strong> at participant x code level.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="question">
            <div class="q-header">
              <span class="q-num">Q10</span>
              <span class="q-role basis">Basis Candidate</span>
            </div>
            <p class="q-text">"What frustrates you most about your bank? Where does it fall short?"</p>
