    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION: Magnitude Coding -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="magnitude-coding">
      <h2>Magnitude Coding Methodology</h2>

      <p><strong>Method:</strong> Magnitude coding (Saldana, 2021). <strong>Dimensions:</strong> Direction and intensity (Saldana's exact terminology). Magnitude coding is "the addition of a supplemental alphanumeric or symbolic code to an existing coded datum to indicate its intensity, frequency, direction, presence, or evaluative content." We apply two of these dimensions.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Why direction and intensity?</strong> These two dimensions answer the two questions most relevant to marketing and product teams: <strong>Direction</strong> tells you "is this an asset or a liability?" (maps to Keller's 1993 favorability dimension). <strong>Intensity</strong> tells you "how much does it matter to behavior?" (Singh 1988: complaint intensity predicts exit vs. voice vs. loyalty; Zeelenberg &amp; Pieters 2004: same negative direction but different intensity = different behavioral responses).
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-grid">
        <div class="comparison-card b2c">
          <h3>Direction (+/-/~)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Level:</strong> Meaning-unit level, during dual-coder phase</li>
            <li><strong>Applied to:</strong> EVERY meaning unit (even question-directed ones)</li>
            <li><strong>+ Positive:</strong> Approval, satisfaction, benefit</li>
            <li><strong>- Negative:</strong> Disapproval, criticism, complaint</li>
            <li><strong>~ Mixed/Neutral:</strong> Factual or hedged; default for ambiguous</li>
            <li><strong>Critical rule:</strong> Reflects participant's stance, not topic nature. "I love their fees" = +, "Their fees are outrageous" = -</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="comparison-question">Research: Osgood et al. (1956), Krippendorff (2018), Keller (1993)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-card b2c">
          <h3>Intensity (1/2/3)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Level:</strong> Participant x code level, post-coding aggregation</li>
            <li><strong>Why not meaning-unit:</strong> Our meaning units are too micro; intensity manifests across totality</li>
            <li><strong>1 Brief:</strong> Single generic statement, no detail</li>
            <li><strong>2 Elaborated:</strong> 2+ meaning units OR specific detail, mild intensifiers</li>
            <li><strong>3 Narrative:</strong> Incident story, strong language, emotional markers, or returns to topic unprompted</li>
            <li><strong>Why 3 levels:</strong> Saldana recommends 3-4; finer distinctions are unreliable</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="comparison-question">Research: Singh (1988), Zeelenberg &amp; Pieters (2004), Miles et al. (2020)</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Why Direction on ALL Meaning Units?</h3>
      <p>Even when a question implies direction (e.g., "What frustrates you?" implies negative), each meaning unit gets an explicit direction code. Rationale: meaning units are queried independently of their source question in downstream analysis. If a meaning unit from a frustration question is later retrieved in a cross-question query, the source question context is gone. Without an explicit direction tag, the evaluative dimension is lost.</p>
      <p>For question-directed responses (frustrations = negative, retained value = positive), direction coding is fast and nearly automatic. For open-ended responses (CEP associations, origin stories), the coder reads context and makes a judgment.</p>

      <h3>Why NOT Emotional Coding?</h3>
      <p>We considered and rejected emotional coding (coding for specific emotions: anger, joy, anxiety) for three reasons: (1) lower inter-rater reliability because it requires inferring internal states, (2) disproportionate complexity (6-20 categories per meaning unit), (3) our research questions don't require it. We need to know favorable vs. unfavorable (direction) and how strongly held (intensity), not whether a negative association produces anger specifically vs. disappointment.</p>

      <h3>Three Dimensions Per Meaning Unit</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Level</th><th>Values</th><th>Applied When</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Thematic code</strong></td><td>Meaning-unit</td><td>Codebook codes</td><td>Existing pipeline</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Direction</strong></td><td>Meaning-unit</td><td>+ / - / ~</td><td>During dual-coder application (Phase 1a)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Intensity</strong></td><td>Participant x code</td><td>1 / 2 / 3</td><td>Post-coding aggregation (Phase 2)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Pipeline-level improvement:</strong> Magnitude coding applies to both B2C and B2B studies. All future studies should use direction + intensity coding. The existing extraction, clustering, and application pipeline is unchanged; direction is added during application, and intensity is a new post-coding pass.
      </div>

      <h3>What This Produces in the Report</h3>
      <p>"62% of non-customers mentioned fees (direction: negative). Of those, 35% were intensity 1 (brief mention), 40% were intensity 2 (elaborated), and 25% were intensity 3 (specific incident narrative). The intensity-3 group overlaps 2.5x more with participants who reported seriously considering switching (Q12), identifying fee frustration at the narrative level as a genuine switching trigger, not background noise."</p>
    </section>

