    <!-- SECTION: Buyer/Non-Buyer -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="buyer-nonbuyer">
      <h2>Buyer/Non-Buyer Analysis Framework</h2>

      <p><strong>Why this is non-negotiable:</strong> Growth comes from non-buyers. A brand's current customers already buy from it. Measuring their awareness tells you about retention. Measuring NON-customers' awareness tells you about growth potential.</p>

      <div class="callout foundation-callout">
        <strong>BBH is emphatic across multiple chapters:</strong><br>
        Ch. 2: "Brand buyers have a higher baseline Brand Awareness score due to past brand buying. To detect whether marketing communications successfully built Brand Awareness among a brand's non-buyers, we would need to quarantine brand buyer responses."<br>
        Ch. 6: "MPen is most useful among non-buyers of brands, as this is where their potential to become future buyers will be first detected."<br>
        Ch. 4: Modified wording forms suppress non-buyer responses by 27% vs. 14% for buyers. Non-buyers are 2x more affected by measurement choices.
      </div>

      <h3>How It Works</h3>
      <p>Q6 (primary bank) classifies each participant. For any given brand: <strong>brand buyers</strong> = participants whose primary bank is this brand (30-60 people), <strong>brand non-buyers</strong> = everyone else (140-170 people).</p>

      <h3>What Each Split Tells You</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Analysis</th><th>Buyer Data = Retention Health</th><th>Non-Buyer Data = Growth Potential</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>CEP-brand associations</strong></td><td>Which situations customers associate with the brand (reflects experience)</td><td>Which situations NON-customers associate with the brand (reflects mental availability)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Spontaneous awareness</strong></td><td>Near-ceiling; not diagnostic</td><td>The brand's presence in the growth audience's memory</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Frustrations</strong></td><td>What might cause churn (retention risk)</td><td>What non-customers believe is wrong (acquisition barrier)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Rejection</strong></td><td>Rarely applicable</td><td>Low rejection + high unawareness (Ehrenberg-Bass prediction)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Direction of associations</strong></td><td>Expected to skew positive</td><td>Reveals brand's reputation among the growth audience</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Report rule:</strong> Every chart showing brand-level data includes a buyer/non-buyer breakdown. The non-buyer row is the strategic row for marketing teams. Recommendations separate retention actions (buyer data) from acquisition actions (non-buyer data).
      </div>
    </section>

