              <span class="chart-name">Top Market Challenges (with intensity)</span>
              <span class="chart-source">Q15</span>
            </div>
            <div class="chart-item">
              <span class="chart-type">Bar</span>
              <span class="chart-name">Ideal Experience Themes</span>
              <span class="chart-source">Q16</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="report-section-card">
          <div class="rs-header">
            <span class="rs-num">11</span>
            <h4>Consumer Language Bank</h4>
            <span class="rs-badge">New</span>
          </div>
          <p>Curated verbatims organized by theme, CEP, and segment. Tagged with direction, intensity, and buyer/non-buyer status. Direct input for ad copywriters and content creators. "Use these words, not those words."</p>
          <div class="chart-list">
            <div class="chart-item">
              <span class="chart-type">Table</span>
              <span class="chart-name">Language by Theme (buyer/non-buyer)</span>
              <span class="chart-source">All questions</span>
            </div>
            <div class="chart-item">
              <span class="chart-type">Table</span>
              <span class="chart-name">Language by CEP (buyer/non-buyer)</span>
              <span class="chart-source">Q3 + Q4</span>
            </div>
            <div class="chart-item">
              <span class="chart-type">Table</span>
              <span class="chart-name">Language by Segment</span>
              <span class="chart-source">All questions</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>

      <h3>Appendix: What to Track Next</h3>
      <p>Recommendations for a future tracker or repeat study: which CEPs to monitor, three-tier awareness per brand among non-buyers, frustration intensity distributions over time, segment stability, multi-banking rates, and digital challenger growth as secondary providers.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION: Recommendations -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="recommendations">
      <h2>Recommendations by Stakeholder</h2>

      <p>The three-audience framework maps to three decision types. <strong>All recommendations split by retention actions (buyer data) and acquisition actions (non-buyer data).</strong></p>

      <div class="stakeholder-grid">

        <!-- Brand & Marketing -->
        <div class="stakeholder-card">
          <div class="stakeholder-header marketing-bg">
            <h3>Brand &amp; Marketing</h3>
            <p class="stakeholder-who">CMO, Brand VP, Advertising, Content</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stakeholder-body">
            <p class="stakeholder-decision">Decisions: <strong>What to say and where to say it</strong></p>

            <h4>Strategic Recommendations</h4>

            <div class="rec-item">
              <h5>CEP Prioritization Strategy (Using Non-Buyer Data)</h5>
              <p>Identify 2-3 CEPs where the client should concentrate advertising. Prioritize using the non-buyer rows of the CEP-Brand Association Map: CEPs where the client has low non-buyer linkage but the CEP is high-relevance (white space), or moderate linkage with no competitor dominance (contestable).</p>
              <p class="rec-grounding">Romaniuk &amp; Sharp (2022): Brands grow mental availability by building more CEP linkages, not deepening existing ones.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="rec-item">
              <h5>Awareness Over Persuasion</h5>
              <p>If the data confirms low rejection and high unawareness among non-buyers (per Ehrenberg-Bass prediction), the priority is building basic awareness and CEP linkage with the unaware population, not campaigns to change the few rejecters' minds.</p>
              <p class="rec-grounding">Rejection averages 7-11%. Unawareness is 4-16x larger. ROI on converting unaware to aware exceeds converting rejecters.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="rec-item">
              <h5>Reach the Whole Market (95-5 Rule)</h5>
              <p>Most consumers are not actively looking for a new bank. Broad-reach advertising builds memory structures (CEP-brand links) that determine which bank comes to mind when a life event triggers a need months or years later.</p>
              <p class="rec-grounding">Sharp (2010): Advertising effectiveness in long-cycle categories depends on memory-building, not immediate conversion.</p>
            </div>

            <h4>Tactical Recommendations</h4>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Messaging by CEP:</strong> Using consumer language from the Consumer Language Bank (Section 11), differentiated for buyer (retention) vs. non-buyer (acquisition) audiences</li>
              <li><strong>Segment-specific campaign briefs:</strong> One page per segment with CEPs, frustrations, tone/language, and channel implications</li>
              <li><strong>Competitive positioning:</strong> Based on CEP Competitive Battleground (non-buyer data) for acquisition strategy</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Product & Digital -->
        <div class="stakeholder-card">
          <div class="stakeholder-header product-bg">
            <h3>Product &amp; Digital</h3>
            <p class="stakeholder-who">Head of Digital, Product Managers, UX</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stakeholder-body">
            <p class="stakeholder-decision">Decisions: <strong>What to build</strong></p>

            <h4>Strategic Recommendations</h4>

            <div class="rec-item">
              <h5>Close Frustration Gaps That Cause Switching (Intensity-Weighted)</h5>
              <p>Cross-reference frustrations (Section 6) with switching triggers (Section 6e cross-reference chart). Frustrations at intensity 3 that also appear in switching stories are the product priorities. Frustrations at intensity 1 that never trigger switching are lower priority, regardless of frequency.</p>
              <p class="rec-grounding">JTBD framework + Singh (1988): complaint intensity predicts exit vs. voice vs. loyalty. Frequency alone misleads (Miles et al. 2020).</p>
            </div>

            <div class="rec-item">
              <h5>Stem Multi-Banking Leakage</h5>
              <p>Section 8 identifies where consumers go for secondary products and why. Each leakage category = product gap to close or bundling opportunity.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="rec-item">
              <h5>Physical Availability Still Matters</h5>
              <p>If proximity-driven origin pathways remain significant, branch presence is still a growth factor. For digital challengers winning younger cohorts, investment shifts to app experience and digital onboarding.</p>
              <p class="rec-grounding">Sharp (2010): Physical availability is the second growth pillar alongside mental availability.</p>
            </div>

            <h4>Tactical Recommendations</h4>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Feature priority matrix:</strong> Unmet needs (Q11, Q13, Q16) against competitive offerings, weighted by intensity</li>
              <li><strong>Digital experience gap analysis:</strong> Features non-customers associate with challengers vs. client offering</li>
              <li><strong>Segment-specific product needs:</strong> Frustrations and unmet needs per segment with intensity distributions</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Retail & Growth -->
        <div class="stakeholder-card">
          <div class="stakeholder-header growth-bg">
            <h3>Retail &amp; Growth</h3>
            <p class="stakeholder-who">Head of Retail, Acquisition, Retention, Branch Network</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stakeholder-body">
            <p class="stakeholder-decision">Decisions: <strong>Who to acquire and how to keep them</strong></p>

            <h4>Strategic Recommendations</h4>

            <div class="rec-item">
              <h5>Penetration, Not Loyalty</h5>
              <p>Growth comes from acquiring more customers, not deepening loyalty. Loyalty metrics are largely determined by brand size (Double Jeopardy). Primary growth investment should be acquisition.</p>
              <p class="rec-grounding">Double Jeopardy confirmed across consumer banking categories. Smaller brands have fewer customers AND lower loyalty.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="rec-item">
              <h5>Be Present at Life Events</h5>
              <p>Life events are the primary triggers putting consumers in-market. Acquisition strategy should concentrate on these moments through partnerships, targeted media, and CEP-linked messaging.</p>
              <p class="rec-grounding">JTBD: push forces accumulate but a triggering event (almost always a life event in banking) is required to overcome habit and anxiety.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="rec-item">
              <h5>Lower Switching Barriers as Acquisition Tool</h5>
              <p>Section 7 quantifies barriers. If "too much hassle to re-set up payments" is dominant, an acquisition program that automates the switch removes the largest obstacle.</p>
            </div>

            <h4>Tactical Recommendations</h4>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Competitor vulnerability map:</strong> Cross-reference frustrations (Q10, intensity-weighted), switching propensity (Q14), and non-buyer awareness of client (Q2/Q4)</li>
              <li><strong>Segment acquisition prioritization:</strong> Rank by size, client under-representation, switching propensity, awareness</li>
              <li><strong>Retention risk dashboard:</strong> Client's customers by segment, with leaving propensity and intensity-3 frustrations</li>
              <li><strong>Onboarding priorities:</strong> New customers (short tenure in Q6): what they value and what frustrates them</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION: Segmentation -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="segmentation">
      <h2>Segmentation Approach</h2>

      <p>In B2B, basis variables came from the evaluation process (triggers, criteria, decision authority). In B2C, basis variables shift because most consumers never went through a formal evaluation. Terminology follows Wedel &amp; Kamakura (2000) exactly.</p>

      <h3>Primary Basis Candidates</h3>
      <div class="basis-grid">
        <div class="basis-card">
          <h4>Bank Origin Type</h4>
          <p>Inherited / active choice / life event / proximity / other</p>
          <p class="basis-source">From Q7</p>
        </div>
        <div class="basis-card">
          <h4>Multi-Banking Behavior</h4>
          <p>Single provider vs. split across multiple</p>
          <p class="basis-source">From Q8</p>
        </div>
        <div class="basis-card">
          <h4>CEP Importance Profile</h4>
          <p>Which category entry points matter most to this consumer</p>
          <p class="basis-source">From Q4</p>
        </div>
        <div class="basis-card">
          <h4>Frustration Patterns</h4>
          <p>If they show cross-participant variation independent of bank choice</p>
          <p class="basis-source">From Q10 (basis candidate)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="basis-card">
          <h4>Switching Force Balance</h4>
          <p>Push/pull vs. anxiety/habit dominance</p>
          <p class="basis-source">From Q12 (basis candidate)</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Role of Magnitude Coding in Segmentation</h3>
      <p>Direction and intensity are not direct segmentation inputs (they modify codes, not stand alone). They influence segmentation in two ways: (1) <strong>Variable selection:</strong> If a frustration code is 80% intensity-1, only the intensity-3 subset may be worth treating as a basis variable. (2) <strong>Segment profiling:</strong> Direction and intensity distributions within each segment add interpretive depth. "Segments A and B both mention fees, but Segment A is 70% intensity-3 while Segment B is 80% intensity-1."</p>

      <h3>Expected Segment Archetypes (Illustrative)</h3>
      <p>These are hypothetical, not predetermined. Actual segments emerge from the data through MCA + clustering (Wedel &amp; Kamakura, 2000).</p>

      <div class="archetype-grid">
        <div class="archetype-card">
          <h4>Inherited Loyalists</h4>
          <p>Banked with the same institution since childhood. Never considered switching. Low engagement.</p>
          <p class="archetype-strategy"><strong>Growth lever:</strong> Cross-sell, increase share of wallet</p>
        </div>
        <div class="archetype-card">
          <h4>Convenience-First</h4>
          <p>Chose based on proximity or digital ease. Would switch for better convenience. Moderate engagement.</p>
          <p class="archetype-strategy"><strong>Growth lever:</strong> Digital experience, branch network, reduce friction</p>
        </div>
        <div class="archetype-card">
          <h4>Active Optimizers</h4>
          <p>Have switched or use multiple providers. Compare rates and features. Higher financial literacy.</p>
          <p class="archetype-strategy"><strong>Growth lever:</strong> Competitive rates, transparent pricing, reward switching</p>
        </div>
        <div class="archetype-card">
          <h4>Life-Event Movers</h4>
          <p>Banking shaped by life events: immigration, first home, marriage. Needs change with life stage.</p>
          <p class="archetype-strategy"><strong>Growth lever:</strong> Anticipate next life event, be present at the right CEP</p>
        </div>
        <div class="archetype-card">
          <h4>Digitally Displaced</h4>
          <p>Moved some banking to digital challengers but kept a traditional primary account. Splitting wallet.</p>
          <p class="archetype-strategy"><strong>Growth lever:</strong> Match digital rates/experience (traditional); convert to primary (challengers)</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION: Moderation Notes -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="moderation">
      <h2>AI Moderation Notes</h2>

      <div class="moderation-grid">
        <div class="mod-card">
          <h4>Language Calibration</h4>
          <p>Consumers range from financially sophisticated to basic. Use plain language: "savings account" not "deposit product," "mortgage" not "secured lending," "fees" not "service charges." Match the participant's language level.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mod-card">
          <h4>CEP Identification Probing (Q3)</h4>
          <p>Most open-ended part of the interview. Let the participant talk freely, then use 2-3 Ws probes targeted to what they said. Don't run through all 7 mechanically. Push once for completeness. 3-4 min max.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mod-card">
          <h4>CEP Battery Probing (Q4)</h4>
          <p>Present each situation clearly, wait for bank names, probe briefly with "Why does [bank] come to mind for that?" Keep probes brief. Goal: first-mention data and a short reason, not deep exploration.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mod-card">
          <h4>Inherited Relationships (Q7)</h4>
          <p>Many will say "I've been with [bank] since I was a kid." Don't push for an evaluation story that doesn't exist. The absence of deliberate choice is itself data.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mod-card">
          <h4>Switching Story Depth (Q12)</h4>
          <p>Spend extra time with the ~20-30% who switched (the JTBD gold). For non-switchers, keep Q12 briefer: 1-2 probes on retention anchors.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mod-card">
          <h4>Time Management</h4>
          <p>Section 1 (Mental Landscape) is the time risk. If running long, reduce Ws probes in Q3 to 2 and CEP battery in Q4 from 5 to 4. Never cut Q3/Q4 in favor of later questions. The CEP data is the strategically most valuable output.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mod-card">
          <h4>Anchoring Bias Prevention</h4>
          <p>Do NOT mention any bank names before Q6. In Q3 and Q4, let the participant generate names spontaneously. If they ask "which banks do you mean?", respond "whichever banks come to mind for you."</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</InternalLayout>

